To build forms easier, and in a way that resembles normal rails forms, tou can add the following
form builder to your project:

```rb
class FormBuilder < ActionView::Helpers::FormBuilder
  def method_missing(method_name, *args, **kwargs, &block)
    super and return unless method_name =~ /_component$/

    component_name = method_name.to_s.gsub("_component", "")
    method_class = "Form::#{component_name.camelize}".constantize

    self.class.define_method(method_name) do |name, *args, **kw_args, &block|
      method_class.new(*args, name: name, form: self, **kw_args)
                                  .render_in(@template, &block)
    end

    send(method_name, *args, **kwargs, &block)
  rescue NameError
    raise NameError.new("missing form component: Form::#{component_name.camelize}")
  end

  def respond_to_missing?(method_name, include_private = false)
    method_name =~ /_component$/ || super
  end
end
```

Also, you can add a helper to instantiate the form builder:

```rb
module ViewComponentHelper
  def component_form_with(**options, &block)
    form_with(**options.merge(builder: FormBuilder), &block)
  end
end
```

The builder expects to get sent the name of the field as the first argument, and it will pass
any further arguments directly to the component.

```erb
<%%= component_form_with model: @user do |form| %>
  <%%= form.text_field_component :email, type: :email %>
<%% end %>
```

To add new field components, you need to create them inside of the `Form` module for the builder
to pick them up.
